                      "name": "leaf",
                      "value": "In Shakespeare's day, English grammar, spelling, and pronunciation were less standardised than they are now,[223] and his use of language helped shape modern English.[224] Samuel Johnson quoted him more often than any other author in his A Dictionary of the English Language, the first serious work of its type.[225] Expressions such as 'with bated breath' (Merchant of Venice) and 'a foregone conclusion' (Othello) have found their way into everyday English speech",
